# If you want to see the complete list of every file Git is currently tracking, run:
# git ls-files
#
# And if you ever want to double-check whether a specific file inside <filepath> would be ignored if you created a new file there, run:
# git check-ignore -v <filepath>
# It is a test command that asks Git what it would do with a file path, even if that file does not exist yet on your computer. Git checks
# <filepath> against your .gitignore file from top to bottom and gives you one of two results:
# * Result 1: Blank output (No text returned)
#   * What it means: The file is NOT ignored. Your ! rules worked, and Git will track this file when you create it.
# * Result 2: Prints a file and line number (for example: .gitignore:2:*    <filepath>)
#   * What it means: The file IS ignored. It tells you the exact file (.gitignore), line number (2), and rule (*) that is blocking it.
#
# You can test which files Git will track before committing by running:
# git status --ignored -uall
#
# Ignore everything.
*

# But include these...
!.vscode
!.vscode/**
!.gitignore
!.meta
!LICENSE
!README.md
